      "message": {
        "content": "{\n  \"review_title\": \"Gut Microbiome Modulation of Immune Checkpoint Inhibitor Efficacy in Solid Tumours: Mechanisms and Clinical Evidence\",\n  \"central_question\": \"How does the gut microbiome modulate the efficacy of immune checkpoint inhibitors in solid tumours, and what mechanisms mediate that effect?\",\n  \"provisional_scope\": \"This review synthesizes evidence on the role of the gut microbiome in modulating immune checkpoint inhibitor (ICI) efficacy across solid tumours. It covers clinical associations (antibiotic exposure, FMT outcomes), microbial signatures (taxa, metabolites), mechanistic pathways (immune modulation, metabolic signaling, barrier integrity), and therapeutic interventions. The scope excludes hematological malignancies unless mechanistic insights are directly transferable to solid tumours. Emphasis is placed on human clinical data over preclinical models, with explicit notation of evidence grades (observational vs. interventional).\",\n  \"material_theme_inventory\": [\n    {\n      \"theme\": \"Clinical Evidence & Antibiotic Impact\",\n      \"sources\": {\n        \"redacted\": true,\n        \"original_bytes\": 27,\n        \"sha256\": \"994ab9b6f79f8daa5fea9d80010b75472228a91bc094dab4f894a9a50be091c9\"\n      },\n      \"key_findings\": \"Antibiotic exposure timing (pre- vs. post-ICI) significantly impacts survival; specific taxa (Fusicatenibacter, Butyricicoccus) associate with better OS in NSCLC.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"theme\": \"FMT Efficacy & Safety\",\n      \"sources\": {\n        \"redacted\": true,\n        \"original_bytes\": 27,\n        \"sha256\": \"aab54c1edebbb682b7f99506291ff0f2374a5aec3e700ae0bf5c44acaf68a1e5\"\n      },\n      \"key_findings\": \"Phase I/II trials show safety and potential efficacy (ORR up to 80% in NSCLC); mechanisms may involve pathobiont clearance rather than donor engraftment; safety risks (irAEs) vary by regimen.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"theme\": \"Metabolite Mechanisms\",\n      \"sources\": {\n        \"redacted\": true,\n        \"original_bytes\": 27,\n        \"sha256\": \"5e690bc141e8db3d59f1efcb9ac23f1404996a2d007f72b43ce0033a9f4d53fa\"\n      },\n      \"key_findings\": \"Inosine, SCFAs, and bile acids modulate T cell function and tumor immunogenicity; direct human TME concentration data is limited.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"theme\": \"Immune Modulation Pathways\",\n      \"sources\": {\n        \"redacted\": true,\n        \"original_bytes\": 27,\n        \"sha256\": \"c50d21cfb19a399c3c7574e5c1f6fa2a6412719201756a58f9f25050177a4b43\"\n      },\n      \"key_findings\": \"Microbiome influences DC maturation, T cell priming, and myeloid cell suppression; ribosome biogenesis in host cells also plays a role.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"theme\": \"Interventions & Translation\",\n      \"sources\": {\n        \"redacted\": true,\n        \"original_bytes\": 27,\n        \"sha256\": \"abc97e9a7eb657500d85e9d0a3949cecc34bc8ea84fe418ba9e91c7d061af94c\"\n      },\n      \"key_findings\": \"FMT, probiotics, and diet show promise but lack standardization; Phase III RCT data is currently absent.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"provisional_outline\": {\n    \"chapters\": [\n      {\n        \"chapter_id\": \"CH01\",\n        \"title\": \"Clinical Landscape: Antibiotics, FMT, and Observational Data\",\n        \"purpose\": \"Evaluate the strength of clinical evidence linking microbiome perturbations to ICI outcomes.\",\n        \"question\": \"What is the current state of clinical evidence regarding microbiome modulation of ICI efficacy?\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"chapter_id\": \"CH02\",\n        \"title\": \"Microbial Signatures: Taxa and Metabolites\",\n        \"purpose\": \"Identify specific microbial features associated with response or resistance.\",\n        \"question\": \"Which specific taxa and metabolites predict ICI response in solid tumours?\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"chapter_id\": \"CH03\",\n        \"title\": \"Mechanistic Pathways: Immune, Metabolic, and Barrier Functions\",\n        \"purpose\": \"Elucidate the biological mechanisms mediating microbiome-ICI interactions.\",\n        \"question\": \"What molecular and cellular mechanisms mediate the effect of the microbiome on ICI efficacy?\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"chapter_id\": \"CH04\",\n        \"title\": \"Therapeutic Interventions: FMT, Probiotics, and Diet\",\n        \"purpose\": \"Assess the feasibility and efficacy of microbiome-targeted interventions.\",\n        \"question\": \"Can microbiome interventions reliably enhance ICI efficacy in clinical practice?\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"chapter_id\": \"CH05\",\n        \"title\": \"Limitations, Heterogeneity, and Future Directions\",\n        \"purpose\": \"Address evidence gaps, methodological challenges, and research priorities.\",\n        \"question\": \"What are the major limitations in current research and what is needed for clinical translation?\"\n      }\n    ]\n  },\n  \"cross_chapter_rules\": [\n    \"Distinguish between observational associations (antibiotics) and interventional data (FMT) when discussing efficacy.\",\n    \"Explicitly note species differences (mouse vs. human) and tumor type specificity (e.g., melanoma vs. NSCLC) in all mechanistic claims.\",\n    \"Do not generalize metabolite effects (e.g., butyrate) without specifying context (ICI type, concentration, tumor type).\",\n    \"Highlight the absence of Phase III RCTs in Chapters 1 and 5 to manage expectations about clinical readiness.\"\n  ],\n  \"grouped_screening_notes\": [\n    \"Exclude studies focused solely on hematological malignancies unless mechanistic insights are explicitly linked to solid tumour pathways.\",\n    \"Prioritize human clinical data for efficacy claims; use preclinical data primarily for mechanistic explanation.\",\n    \"Exclude reviews that do not provide specific mechanistic details or clinical data summaries.\",\n    \"Flag studies lacking adjustment for clinical confounders (e.g., infection severity, performance status) when citing antibiotic effects.\"\n  ],\n  \"supplement_requests\": [\n    {\n      \"gap_id\": \"case_E_phase3_rct\",\n      \"gap_question\": \"Are there any completed large-scale Phase III randomized controlled trials of microbiome interventions in ICI-treated solid tumour patients?\",\n      \"success_criteria\": \"Identification of a completed Phase III RCT with published primary outcome data on efficacy (OS/PFS/ORR).\",\n      \"chapter_ids\": [\n        \"CH01\",\n        \"CH05\"\n      ],\n      \"intended_use\": \"background\",\n      \"targeted_queries\": \"phase III randomized controlled trial fecal microbiota transplantation immune checkpoint inhibitor solid tumor\",\n      \"known_papers\": [\n        \"P0577\",\n        \"P0578\",\n        \"P0583\",\n        \"P0584\"\n      ],\n      \"reviewed_references\": []\n    },\n    {\n      \"gap_id\": \"case_C2_inosine_tme\",\n      \"gap_question\": \"What are the measured physiological concentrations of inosine and SCFAs inside the human tumour microenvironment correlating with ICI response?\",\n      \"success_criteria\": \"Quantitative data (e.g., μM, ng/mL) of specific metabolites in human tumour tissue or serum linked to ICI outcomes.\",\n      \"chapter_ids\": [\n        \"CH02\",\n        \"CH03\"\n      ],\n      \"intended_use\": \"mechanism\",\n      \"targeted_queries\": \"inosine concentration human tumour microenvironment immune checkpoint inhibitor response metabolomics\",\n      \"known_papers\": [\n        \"P0580\",\n        \"P0581\",\n        \"P0582\"\n      ],\n      \"reviewed_references\": []\n    }\n  ],\n  \"directed_reads\": [\n    {\n      \"paper_id\": \"P0575\",\n      \"chapter_ids\": [\n        \"CH01\"\n      ],\n      \"questions\": [\n        \"How does antibiotic timing (pre- vs. post-ICI) specifically affect overall survival across different tumour types?\"\n      ],\n      \"required_outputs\": \"Extract adjusted hazard ratios for OS by exposure window and tumour subtype.\",\n      \"knowledge_gap\": \"ici_antibiotic_exposure_outcomes\",\n      \"reason\": \"Provides large-scale observational data on antibiotic timing impact, crucial for clinical context.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"paper_id\": \"P0582\",\n      \"chapter_ids\": [\n        \"CH03\"\n      ],\n      \"questions\": [\n        \"What is the specific molecular mechanism by which inosine enhances tumour immunogenicity?\"\n      ],\n      \"required_outputs\": \"Summarize the UBA6 inhibition pathway and its effect on T cell sensitivity.\",\n      \"knowledge_gap\": \"specific_metabolite_quantification\",\n      \"reason\": \"Offers detailed mechanistic insight into metabolite-mediated modulation, despite lacking human TME concentration data.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"paper_id\": \"P0583\",\n      \"chapter_ids\": [\n        \"CH01\",\n        \"CH04\"\n      ],\n      \"questions\": [\n        \"What were the ORR and safety outcomes of FMT combined with ICI in NSCLC and melanoma?\"\n      ],\n      \"required_outputs\": \"Extract ORR, PFS, OS, and adverse event rates by cohort.\",\n      \"knowledge_gap\": \"large_scale_rct_results\",\n      \"reason\": \"Key Phase II trial data illustrating current efficacy limits and safety profiles of FMT.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"manuscript_parts_plan\": {\n    \"context\": \"Oncologists, immunologists, and translational researchers seeking to understand the current evidence base for microbiome modulation of ICI efficacy in solid tumours, including clinical implications and research gaps.\",\n    \"abstract\": {\n      \"purpose\": \"Provide a concise summary of the review's scope, key findings on modulation and mechanisms, and the current evidence grade.\",\n      \"focus\": [\n        \"Summary of clinical associations (antibiotics, FMT)\",\n        \"Key microbial taxa and metabolites identified\",\n        \"Major mechanistic pathways (immune, metabolic)\",\n        \"Statement on lack of Phase III RCTs\"\n      ],\n      \"boundary\": [\n        \"Exclude detailed methodological descriptions of included studies\",\n        \"Do not present observational associations as causal facts\"\n      ],\n      \"placement\": {\n        \"mode\": \"standalone\",\n        \"anchor\": \"beginning\"\n      },\n      \"finalize_from\": [\n        \"CH01\",\n        \"CH02\",\n        \"CH03\",\n        \"CH05\"\n      ]\n    },\n    \"introduction\": {\n      \"purpose\": \"Define the clinical problem of ICI resistance, introduce the gut microbiome as a modifiable factor, and outline the review's objectives and structure.\",\n      \"focus\": [\n        \"ICI efficacy limitations in solid tumours\",\n        \"Rationale for microbiome research (observational signals)\",\n        \"Review scope (solid tumours, ICIs, gut microbiome)\",\n        \"Outline of chapters\"\n      ],\n      \"boundary\": [\n        \"Do not review basic ICI mechanisms in depth\",\n        \"Exclude hematological malignancies from scope definition\"\n      ],\n      \"placement\": {\n        \"mode\": \"standalone\",\n        \"anchor\": \"beginning\"\n      },\n      \"finalize_from\": [\n        \"CH01\",\n        \"CH05\"\n      ]\n    },\n    \"conclusion\": {\n      \"purpose\": \"Synthesize the strength of evidence, highlight clinical implications (antibiotic stewardship, FMT potential), and emphasize critical research gaps (Phase III trials, metabolite quantification).\",\n      \"focus\": [\n        \"Summary of evidence quality (observational vs. interventional)\",\n        \"Clinical recommendations (cautious antibiotic use)\",\n        \"Research priorities (Phase III RCTs, standardization)\",\n        \"Final statement on translational readiness\"\n      ],\n      \"boundary\": [\n        \"Do not propose unvalidated interventions as standard care\",\n        \"Avoid overstating mechanistic certainty from preclinical data\"\n      ],\n      \"placement\": {\n        \"mode\": \"standalone\",\n        \"anchor\": \"end\"\n      },\n      \"finalize_from\": [\n        \"CH01\",\n        \"CH04\",\n        \"CH05\"\n      ]\n    }\n  }\n}",
        "reasoning_content": "Thinking Process:\n\n1.  **Analyze the Request:**\n    *   **Task:** Create a `provisional_scope` JSON output for a systematic review planning task.\n    *   **Topic:** \"How does the gut microbiome modulate the efficacy of immune checkpoint inhibitors in solid tumours, and what mechanisms mediate that effect?\" (X1_microbiome_ICI)\n    *   **Input:** A large candidate pool of 585 papers (P0001 to P0585) with detailed summaries, facet contributions, scope interpretation cautions, etc.\n    *   **Constraints:**\n        *   Return JSON only.\n        *   Must include specific keys: `review_title`, `central_question`, `provisional_scope`, `material_theme_inventory`, `provisional_outline`, `cross_chapter_rules`, `grouped_screening_notes`, `supplement_requests`, `directed_reads`, `manuscript_parts_plan`.\n        *   `manuscript_parts_plan` must follow a specific schema (`context`, `abstract`, `introduction`, `conclusion` with specific sub-fields like `purpose`, `focus`, `boundary`, `placement`, `finalize_from`).\n        *   `placement.mode` must be `standalone`, `embedded`, or `distributed` (prefer `standalone` if no explicit article location).\n        *   Do not write Q&A lists.\n        *   Do not force use of all sources; screen semantically.\n        *   Do not预设 (preset) a single research paradigm.\n        *   Ensure `focus`/`boundary` are specific to the material and BODY duties, avoiding empty templates.\n        *   Handle `supplement_requests` and `directed_reads` correctly (gap_id, source_handle, etc.).\n        *   Respect `interpretation_limits` from source materials (e.g., observational vs. causal, mouse vs. human, specific cancer types).\n        *   Address the `large_scale_rct_results` gap identified in the supplement material (P0577, P0578, P0583, P0584, P0585 indicate Phase II or narrative reviews, no completed Phase III found yet).\n        *   Address the `specific_metabolite_quantification` gap (P0580, P0581, P0582 indicate mechanisms but lack direct human TME concentration data for inosine/SCFAs).\n        *   Address the `ici_antibiotic_exposure_outcomes` gap (P0575, P0576 provide observational data on timing and outcomes).\n        *   Ensure the outline reflects the material's structure (mechanisms, clinical evidence, interventions, limitations).\n\n2.  **Synthesize Material Theme Inventory:**\n    *   *Clinical Associations:* Antibiotic timing (P0575, P0576), FMT efficacy/safety (P0577, P0578, P0583), Specific taxa (P0565, P0566, P0585), Metabolites (P0559, P0563, P0582).\n    *   *Mechanisms:* Metabolite-mediated (SCFAs, Inosine, Bile Acids) (P0559, P0563, P0582), Immune cell modulation (T cells, DCs, Myeloid) (P0554, P0556, P0580), Barrier integrity/Translocation (P0574).\n    *   *Interventions:* FMT (P0573, P0577, P0578, P0583), Probiotics/Prebiotics (P0567, P0574), Diet (P0550, P0571).\n    *   *Limitations/Heterogeneity:* Lack of Phase III RCTs (P0577, P0578, P0583, P0584), Context-dependency (Tumor type, ICI regimen) (P0566, P0585), Methodological issues (P0565, P0574).\n\n3.  **Draft Provisional Outline:**\n    *   Chapter 1: Clinical Landscape & Evidence Quality (Antibiotics, FMT trials, Observational data).\n    *   Chapter 2: Microbial Taxa & Metabolites (Specific bugs, SCFAs, Inosine, Bile Acids).\n    *   Chapter 3: Mechanistic Pathways (Immune modulation, Barrier function, TME remodeling).\n    *   Chapter 4: Interventions & Translation (FMT, Probiotics, Diet, Safety/Toxicity).\n    *   Chapter 5: Limitations & Future Directions (Phase III gap, Standardization, Personalization).\n\n4.  **Draft Manuscript Parts Plan:**\n    *   `context`: Researchers/Clinicians in oncology/immunology.\n    *   `abstract`: Summarize key findings on modulation and mechanisms.\n    *   `introduction`: Define scope, ICI context, Microbiome relevance.\n    *   `conclusion`: Summarize evidence grade, clinical implications, research gaps.\n    *   *Placement:* `standalone` (as per instruction preference).\n\n5.  **Identify Gaps & Supplement Requests:**\n    *   *Gap 1 (Phase III RCTs):* Material consistently shows Phase I/II or observational. Need to confirm if *any* Phase III completed. (Status: Unmet in search results P0577, P0578, P0583, P0584). -> `supplement_requests` needed.\n    *   *Gap 2 (Metabolite Quantification):* Lack of direct human TME concentration data for inosine/SCFAs correlating with response. (Status: Unmet in P0580, P0581, P0582). -> `supplement_requests` needed.\n    *   *Gap 3 (Antibiotic Confounding):* Observational data exists, but causal adjustment details vary. (Status: Partially met in P0575, P0576). -> Can be used but noted as observational.\n\n6.  **Refine Screening Notes:**\n    *   Exclude non-solid tumors (hematological) unless mechanistic overlap is clear.\n    *   Exclude reviews that don't offer mechanistic depth or specific clinical data.\n    *   Prioritize human clinical data over mouse models for efficacy claims.\n\n7.  **Construct JSON:** Ensure all fields match the schema exactly. Check `manuscript_parts_plan` structure carefully.\n\n    *   *Self-Correction on `manuscript_parts_plan`:* The prompt requires `context` (string), `abstract`, `introduction`, `conclusion`. Each part needs `purpose`, `focus`, `boundary`, `placement`, `finalize_from`. `placement` needs `mode` and `anchor`. `mode` should be `standalone`.\n    *   *Self-Correction on `supplement_requests`:* Need to define `gap_id`, `gap_question`, `success_criteria`, `chapter_ids`, `intended_use`, `targeted_queries`.\n    *   *Self-Correction on `directed_reads`:* Need `paper_id`, `chapter_ids`, `questions`, `required_outputs`, `knowledge_gap`, `reason`.\n\n8.  **Final Review against Constraints:**\n    *   JSON only.\n    *   No Q&A lists.\n    *   Respect source limits (e.g., P0577 is Phase I, not Phase III).\n    *   Ensure `central_question` matches the topic.\n    *   Ensure `provisional_scope` defines boundaries (Solid tumors, ICIs, Gut Microbiome).\n\n    *Wait, one detail:* The prompt says \"Return JSON only.\" and \"Every source request must name source_handle...\".\n    *Also:* \"manuscript_parts_plan must be a complete object... context plus abstract/introduction/conclusion.\"\n    *Also:* \"placement {mode: standalone|embedded|distributed, anchor: semantic location}\".\n    *Also:* \"If no explicit article location is confirmed, prefer standalone\".\n\n    *Let's build the JSON.*\n\n    *Review Title:* Gut Microbiome Modulation of Immune Checkpoint Inhibitor Efficacy in Solid Tumours: Mechanisms and Clinical Evidence.\n    *Central Question:* How does the gut microbiome modulate the efficacy of immune checkpoint inhibitors in solid tumours, and what mechanisms mediate that effect?\n    *Scope:* Solid tumours, ICIs (PD-1/PD-L1, CTLA-4), Gut Microbiome (taxa, metabolites), Clinical outcomes (ORR, PFS, OS, irAEs), Mechanisms (immune, metabolic, barrier).\n    *Outline:* 1. Clinical Evidence (Antibiotics, FMT), 2. Microbial Signatures (Taxa, Metabolites), 3. Mechanisms (Immune, Metabolic), 4. Interventions (FMT, Diet), 5. Limitations (RCT gap, Heterogeneity).\n    *Manuscript Parts:* Context (Oncologists/Researchers), Abstract (Summary), Intro (Background), Conclusion (Implications/Gaps).\n    *Supplement Requests:* Phase III RCTs, Human TME Metabolite Concentrations.\n    *Directed Reads:* Specific papers for mechanism details (e.g., P0582 for Inosine, P0575 for Antibiotics).\n\n    *Check `manuscript_parts_plan` schema again:*\n    `context`: string.\n    `abstract`, `introduction`, `conclusion`: objects with `purpose` (string), `focus` (string[]), `boundary` (string[]), `placement` ({mode, anchor}), `finalize_from` (string[]).\n    `placement.mode`: `standalone`.\n    `placement.anchor`: e.g., \"beginning\", \"end\".\n\n    *Check `supplement_requests`:*\n    `gap_id`, `gap_question`, `success_criteria`, `chapter_ids`, `intended_use`, `targeted_queries`, `optional reuse_plan_facet_ids`, `known_papers`, `reviewed_references`.\n\n    *Check `directed_reads`:*\n    `paper_id`, `chapter_ids`, `questions`, `required_outputs`, `knowledge_gap`, `reason`.\n\n    *Let's assemble.*",
